  "prompt_text": "The figure gives a simple day–night overview of a bioelectrochemical system that couples solar energy storage to electricity generation. The left half, shown in daylight, represents an “energy storage” step. Sunlight drives microbial activity in a chamber containing bacteria-like cells, with wavy arrows indicating incoming light and small negative signs and an electron label suggesting charge transfer or storage of reducing power in the system.\n\nThe right half, shown at night, represents “power generation.” Material or stored energy from the daytime chamber appears to feed a second chamber with an electrode. There, microbes transfer electrons to the electrode, and the electrons flow through an external circuit to produce electrical power. Inside the chamber, oxygen, protons, and water are labeled, indicating oxygen reduction to water as part of the electrochemical reaction.\n\nOverall, the image illustrates a two-stage concept: solar energy is first captured and stored biologically during the day, then later converted into usable electrical current in the dark through microbial electron transfer and an electrode-based circuit. The central sun–moon graphic reinforces this temporal separation between charging and discharging.",
